WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.135
Default credentials and sample accounts remain dangerous because attackers know where to look for them.

2
00:00:07.135 --> 00:00:15.240
Any default identity, password, token, or administrative endpoint should be removed or changed before production use.

3
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:28.332
Open cloud storage, public management interfaces, permissive network rules, and exposed dashboards can turn a configuration oversight into direct data exposure or administrative compromise.

4
00:00:28.332 --> 00:00:31.449
Unnecessary services increase attack surface.

5
00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:41.355
Every enabled port, plugin, feature flag, debugging endpoint, and administrative function should have a business reason and a documented owner.

6
00:00:41.355 --> 00:00:48.974
Debug mode exposure can leak stack traces, environment variables, credentials, paths, and framework internals.

7
00:00:48.974 --> 00:00:53.685
Detailed diagnostics belong in protected logs, not public responses.

8
00:00:53.685 --> 00:00:59.157
Use secure baselines for servers, containers, cloud services, and applications.

9
00:00:59.157 --> 00:01:02.967
Apply hardening standards and verify them continuously.

10
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:08.162
Manage configuration through repeatable, reviewed, and monitored processes.

11
00:01:08.162 --> 00:01:17.514
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include CM-2 - Baseline Configuration; CM-6 - Configuration Settings; CM-7 - Least Functionality.

